WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.582
A correlated scalar subquery can appear in SELECT.

00:00:03.582 --> 00:00:08.547
For each patient, the inner query counts admissions for that patient ID.

00:00:08.547 --> 00:00:13.802
COUNT returns one scalar value, so the scalar contract is satisfied.

00:00:13.802 --> 00:00:18.052
The result keeps one row per patient while adding an admission count.

00:00:18.052 --> 00:00:22.777
This form is direct and easy to read for a simple per-row metric.

00:00:22.777 --> 00:00:29.502
IN answers a membership question: is this value present in the set returned by the subquery?

00:00:29.502 --> 00:00:34.537
Be especially careful with NOT IN when NULL can appear in that set,

00:00:34.537 --> 00:00:39.667
because three-valued logic can turn expected TRUE results into UNKNOWN.

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:44.328
For anti-matching, NOT EXISTS is often easier to reason about.
